+## Subtopics
+
+### ProNeRF-8
+The variant with 8 samples. Achieves 26.78 dB PSNR on LLFF, outperforming vanilla NeRF (26.50 dB) by 0.28 dB while being >20x faster [S1].
+
+### ProNeRF-12
+The variant with 12 samples. Achieves 27.15 dB PSNR on LLFF, 0.65 dB higher than vanilla NeRF, while being ~15x faster [S1].
+
+### ProNeRF-32
+Evaluated on Blender dataset, achieving 31.92 dB PSNR and 3.2 FPS after pruning [S1].
+
+### ProNeRF-8 Prune
+Uses a pruning head $F_{\theta_m}$ to predict rendering error. If error is low, uses fast AVR output; otherwise, runs shading network. 23% faster than standard ProNeRF-8 with negligible quality drop [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Speed:** ProNeRF is 15-23× faster than vanilla NeRF [S1]. ProNeRF-8 prune is 23% faster than standard ProNeRF-8 [S1].
-*   **Quality:** ProNeRF-12 achieves 0.65 dB higher PSNR than NeRF on LLFF. ProNeRF-8 achieves 0.28 dB higher PSNR than NeRF [S1]. It outperforms HyperReel by 0.95 dB PSNR [S1].
-*   **Memory:** Similar to NeRF (~3.8 MB for NeRF, ProNeRF-32 is 6.3 MB) [S1]. Pruning adds 3.3 MB [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** High quality with very few samples; consistent memory footprint regardless of scene size (fixed reference views); fast inference.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires calibrated multi-view input; training is complex due to alternating passes and auxiliary losses; performance depends on the quality of the fixed reference view subset [S1].
-*   **When to use:** When rendering speed is critical and high-quality results are needed with limited samples, and calibrated multi-view data is available.
-*   **When not to use:** For uncalibrated or sparse view scenarios where generalizable methods (like IBRNet) might be more robust, or if memory is extremely constrained and pruning overhead is unacceptable.
+*   **Speed:** 15–23× faster than vanilla NeRF [S1]. ProNeRF-8 is >20x faster; ProNeRF-12 is ~15x faster [S1].
+*   **Quality:** 0.65 dB higher PSNR than vanilla NeRF on LLFF; 0.95 dB higher PSNR than HyperReel [S1].
+*   **Memory:** Similar to NeRF. Pruning adds 3.3 MB overhead [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** Uses a fixed subset of reference views, ensuring consistent memory footprint regardless of total training views [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** High speed, high quality, efficient sampling.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires multi-view training data; relies on accurate camera poses for projections [S1].
 
 ## Background
-ProNeRF was proposed in 2024 by the authors of the paper "ProNeRF: Learning Efficient Projection-Aware Ray Sampling for Fine-Grained Implicit Neural Radiance Fields" [S1]. It addresses the trade-off between memory, speed, and quality in neural rendering, aiming to outperform sampler-based methods like HyperReel and grid-based methods like K-Planes [S1]. It builds on the principles of sparse and efficient representations [Parent].
+ProNeRF was proposed in 2024 by the authors of the source paper [S1]. It addresses the quality degradation in sampler-based methods and the memory issues in generalizable radiance fields by using a projection-aware sampling network and an exploration-exploitation training strategy [S1]. It is evaluated on LLFF and Blender datasets [S1].
 
